= Per-concept capture =

== Algorithm ==

'''Current corpus state:''' substantial seed article; currently grounded mainly in NIST.

'''Canonical scope:''' a finite/well-specified effective procedure for solving a computational problem or producing a result. Literature differs on which properties are definitional versus model-specific. Classical definitions often emphasize finiteness/termination; modern literature also discusses interactive, nondeterministic, randomized, online, distributed, and ongoing computational procedures.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* algorithm ≠ program;
* algorithm ≠ heuristic;
* algorithm ≠ computation;
* algorithm specification ≠ implementation;
* correctness ≠ efficiency;
* computability ≠ computational complexity.

'''History to capture:'''
* ancient algorithmic procedures;
* al-Khwarizmi and the etymological lineage of "algorithm";
* 1930s formalization of effective calculability through Church, Turing, Post, Kleene and related work;
* later attempts to characterize algorithms themselves, not merely computable functions.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* input/output;
* state and transition;
* termination/progress;
* correctness;
* invariants;
* complexity/resource use;
* deterministic, randomized, nondeterministic and interactive algorithms;
* abstract-state-machine characterization as one formal theory of sequential algorithms.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* Paul E. Black, "algorithm", NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures. Definition and terminology. https://www.nist.gov/dads/HTML/algorithm.html
* Paul E. Black, ''DADS: The On-Line Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures'', NISTIR 8318 (2020). DOI: 10.6028/NIST.IR.8318.
* Thomas H. Cormen, Charles E. Leiserson, Ronald L. Rivest, Clifford Stein, ''Introduction to Algorithms'', 4th ed., MIT Press (2022). ISBN 9780262046305.
* Yuri Gurevich, "Sequential Abstract-State Machines Capture Sequential Algorithms", ''ACM Transactions on Computational Logic'' 1(1), 77–111 (2000). DOI: 10.1145/343369.343384.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Algorithm|Algorithm]], useful for terminology/history/navigation only, not evidentiary authority.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general "Algorithm" article was found in the current search. Adjacent Scholarpedia coverage exists for algorithmic subfields such as metaheuristics and algorithmic information theory.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* whether termination is stated as part of the Core definition or treated as the classical finite-algorithm convention with modern exceptions;
* whether "heuristic" is defined by lack of guarantee, incomplete search, or broader practical usage.

== Causality ==

'''Current corpus state:''' very thin seed article; currently oriented almost entirely through SEP.

'''Canonical scope:''' the family of concepts and formalisms used to express that one event, condition, process, variable, or intervention makes a difference to another. No single philosophical analysis is uncontested.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* causation ≠ correlation;
* token/actual causation ≠ type/population-level causation;
* causal explanation ≠ prediction;
* cause ≠ background condition;
* direct ≠ mediated effect;
* observational association ≠ intervention;
* philosophical analysis of causation ≠ statistical identification of causal effects.

'''Major theoretical families to represent without collapsing them:'''
* regularity accounts;
* counterfactual accounts;
* probabilistic accounts;
* process/mechanistic accounts;
* intervention/manipulability accounts;
* structural causal models;
* potential-outcomes framework.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* counterfactual dependence;
* interventions;
* confounding;
* causal graphs / structural equations;
* potential outcomes;
* identifiability;
* randomized versus observational studies.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "The Metaphysics of Causation".
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Counterfactual Theories of Causation", substantive revision 2024. https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-counterfactual/
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Causation and Manipulability", substantive revision 2023. https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causation-mani/
* Judea Pearl, ''Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference'', 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press (2009). DOI: 10.1017/CBO9780511803161; ISBN 9780521895606.
* Donald B. Rubin, "Estimating Causal Effects of Treatments in Randomized and Nonrandomized Studies", ''Journal of Educational Psychology'' 66(5), 688–701 (1974). DOI: 10.1037/h0037350.
* Miguel A. Hernán and James M. Robins, ''Causal Inference: What If'' (2020), freely available author edition.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Causality|Causality]], orientation/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general causality article was found in the current search. Causality appears in adjacent Scholarpedia articles, including dynamical-systems discussions, but that is not a substitute for a dedicated causality source.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* whether the Core article should introduce Pearl and potential outcomes equally or defer their formalisms primarily to Technical;
* how much metaphysics belongs in Core before the article becomes inaccessible.

== Computation ==

'''Current corpus state:''' substantial seed article, but still predominantly NIST/CS2023.

'''Canonical scope:''' rule-governed transformation according to a computational model. The article must distinguish abstract computation from concrete physical implementation and from computational modeling of noncomputing systems.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* computation ≠ algorithm;
* computation ≠ program;
* abstract computation ≠ physical/concrete computation;
* computational model ≠ physical computer;
* computability ≠ complexity;
* modeling a phenomenon computationally ≠ showing that the phenomenon itself computes.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* state/configuration;
* transition relation;
* input/output;
* computational model;
* Turing machine and equivalent classical models;
* Church–Turing thesis;
* deterministic, nondeterministic, probabilistic, parallel, distributed and quantum models;
* physical implementation problem.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* Alan M. Turing, "On Computable Numbers, with an Application to the Entscheidungsproblem", ''Proceedings of the London Mathematical Society'' s2-42, 230–265 (1937). DOI: 10.1112/plms/s2-42.1.230.
* NIST DADS, "model of computation". https://www.nist.gov/dads/HTML/modelOfComputation.html
* NIST DADS, "Turing machine". https://www.nist.gov/dads/HTML/turingMachine.html
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Computation in Physical Systems", substantive revision 2025. https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/computation-physicalsystems/
* ACM/IEEE-CS CS2023 knowledge areas for computing foundations.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Computation|Computation]], orientation/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general computation article was found in the current search. Relevant adjacent coverage includes "Computational type theory" and several specialized computational topics.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* where to draw the Core boundary between theoretical computer-science computation and philosophy of physical computation;
* whether the Church–Turing thesis receives a full subsection or primarily links to a future dedicated page.

== Data structure ==

'''Current corpus state:''' substantial seed article with good practical distinctions, but literature base is narrow.

'''Canonical scope:''' a structured organization/representation of data together with relevant relations and operations. Data structure must be distinguished from abstract data type while acknowledging ordinary usage sometimes blurs them.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* data structure ≠ abstract data type;
* logical interface/behavior ≠ physical/concrete representation;
* representation choice affects algorithmic cost;
* worst-case, expected and amortized cost are different;
* main-memory assumptions do not automatically generalize to cache, disk, distributed or concurrent systems.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* values, relations and operations;
* invariants;
* arrays, linked structures, stacks, queues, trees, heaps, hash tables and graphs;
* persistent/immutable structures;
* external-memory and cache-aware structures;
* concurrent and distributed structures;
* correctness of representations.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* NIST DADS, "data structure". https://www.nist.gov/dads/HTML/dataStructure.html
* Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein, ''Introduction to Algorithms'', 4th ed., MIT Press (2022). ISBN 9780262046305.
* Barbara H. Liskov and Stephen N. Zilles, "Programming with Abstract Data Types" (1974), SIGPLAN Symposium on Very High Level Languages, 50–59. DOI: 10.1145/800233.807045.
* C. A. R. Hoare, "Proof of Correctness of Data Representations", ''Acta Informatica'' 1(4), 271–281 (1972).
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Data structure|Data structure]] and [[wikipedia:Abstract data type|Abstract data type]], orientation/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general data-structure article found in the current search.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* whether abstract data type deserves a prerequisite page before this wave is promoted;
* how much modern memory-hierarchy detail belongs in Technical versus future specialized pages.

== Hypothesis ==

'''Current corpus state:''' concise seed article; good basic distinction from theory/prediction but insufficient philosophy/statistics depth.

'''Canonical scope:''' a proposed explanatory or testable claim used in inquiry. "Hypothesis" has importantly different uses in general scientific reasoning and statistical hypothesis testing; these must not be collapsed.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* hypothesis ≠ prediction;
* hypothesis ≠ theory;
* scientific hypothesis ≠ statistical hypothesis;
* testing can disconfirm specific conjunctions of hypothesis + auxiliaries rather than isolate one statement cleanly;
* falsifiability is important historically/philosophically but should not be presented as a complete universal recipe for science.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* hypothesis formation;
* derivation of predictions;
* testability;
* falsification and confirmation;
* auxiliary assumptions;
* null/alternative hypotheses;
* statistical testing as a separate narrower formal practice.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Scientific Method", substantive revision 2026. https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-method/
* National Academies sources on scientific explanation/hypothesis terminology already used in the current page.
* Karl Popper, ''The Logic of Scientific Discovery'' (English edition 1959; original German 1934/35), historically important for falsifiability.
* OpenStax, "The Process of Science", pedagogical secondary source.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Hypothesis|Hypothesis]], orientation/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general hypothesis article found in the current search.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* how to explain Popper without implying all science proceeds by simple falsification;
* whether statistical hypothesis belongs as a linked subsection or only a summary with a separate future article.

== Program (computing) ==

'''Current corpus state:''' substantial practical seed, but source base is mainly NIST/CS2023.

'''Canonical scope:''' an executable or interpretable specification/artifact describing computational behavior in a programming language or machine representation. The page must keep program-as-artifact distinct from process/runtime execution.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* program ≠ algorithm;
* program ≠ process;
* program ≠ software system;
* source program ≠ executable binary;
* programming language ≠ implementation/runtime;
* program correctness is relative to a specification and environment assumptions.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* source / intermediate representation / machine code;
* compilation, interpretation, JIT;
* runtime/process distinction;
* control flow and data flow;
* program semantics;
* concurrency and interaction;
* verification and testing.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* ISO/IEC/IEEE 24765:2017, ''Systems and software engineering — Vocabulary'', current published edition; confirmed in 2022 and now under revision.
* NIST CSRC terminology for code, runtime and correctness proof.
* Glynn Winskel, ''The Formal Semantics of Programming Languages'', MIT Press (1993). DOI: 10.7551/mitpress/3054.001.0001; ISBN 9780262731034.
* ACM/IEEE-CS CS2023 programming-language foundations.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Computer program|Computer program]], orientation/history/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general computer-program article found in the current search.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* whether notebooks, declarative workflows, smart contracts and configuration with executable semantics should be treated as examples or boundary cases;
* exact canonical relation to a future [[Process (computing)]] page.

== Programming language ==

'''Current corpus state:''' strong seed article, but needs deeper formal semantics/history/source breadth.

'''Canonical scope:''' a formal or semi-formal system of notation and rules for expressing programs/computations. Syntax and semantics are central; language identity must be kept distinct from any compiler/interpreter/runtime implementation.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* syntax ≠ semantics;
* static semantics ≠ dynamic semantics;
* language ≠ implementation;
* language ≠ ecosystem;
* "compiled" and "interpreted" often classify implementation strategies, not immutable language kinds;
* programming paradigm labels overlap and are rarely mutually exclusive.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* lexical syntax and grammar;
* static and dynamic semantics;
* type systems;
* binding/scope;
* control structures;
* abstraction mechanisms;
* operational, denotational and axiomatic semantics;
* language implementation;
* paradigms.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* ACM/IEEE-CS CS2023, "Foundations of Programming Languages". https://csed.acm.org/programming-languages/
* Glynn Winskel, ''The Formal Semantics of Programming Languages'', MIT Press (1993). DOI: 10.7551/mitpress/3054.001.0001.
* Shriram Krishnamurthi, ''Programming Languages: Application and Interpretation'', current open textbook lineage. https://www.plai.org/
* official language specifications/reference manuals as examples, not general definitions.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Programming language|Programming language]], orientation/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general programming-language article found in the current search; "Computational type theory" is adjacent technical coverage.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* what belongs in Core versus future pages [[Syntax]], [[Semantics]], [[Type system]], [[Programming paradigm]];
* whether language history receives a dedicated section now or later.

== Software ==

'''Current corpus state:''' substantial seed article; good engineering orientation but concept boundary needs stronger standards/history treatment.

'''Canonical scope:''' programs plus associated information/artifacts required for operation in a defined context. Standards and ordinary usage vary in whether documentation, data, configuration and models are included; the page should present this boundary explicitly rather than force a single universal set.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* software ≠ program;
* software ≠ hardware;
* software ≠ firmware, while boundaries can blur;
* software product/system ≠ source-code repository;
* software engineering ≠ programming;
* software artifact boundary depends on purpose/context.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* architecture and interfaces;
* dependencies;
* configuration;
* lifecycle;
* quality attributes;
* testing/verification;
* deployment/operations;
* maintenance/evolution;
* software supply chain and provenance as later links.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* ISO/IEC/IEEE 24765:2017, ''Systems and software engineering — Vocabulary''.
* IEEE Computer Society, ''Guide to the Software Engineering Body of Knowledge (SWEBOK Guide) V4.0'' (2024), consensus-oriented software-engineering body of knowledge.
* Frederick P. Brooks Jr., "No Silver Bullet—Essence and Accidents of Software Engineering", ''Computer'' 20(4), 10–19 (1987). DOI: 10.1109/MC.1987.1663532.
* NIST CSRC software/hardware terminology.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Software|Software]] and [[wikipedia:Software engineering|Software engineering]], orientation/history/navigation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general software article found in the current search.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* how broadly to define associated data/configuration/documentation without making "software" indistinguishable from an entire sociotechnical system;
* where firmware belongs in the Simple/Core distinction.

== System boundary ==

'''Current corpus state:''' very thin seed article; currently relies mostly on SEBoK.

'''Canonical scope:''' an observer- and purpose-dependent distinction separating a system of interest from its environment for analysis, design, explanation or intervention. The concept should not be reduced to a physical edge.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* analytical boundary ≠ necessarily physical boundary;
* system boundary ≠ interface;
* system-of-interest ≠ environment;
* boundary choice ≠ objective discovery in every context;
* boundary critique concerns the consequences of including/excluding concerns, stakeholders, variables and values.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* observer/purpose dependence;
* system/environment distinction;
* inputs/outputs/interfaces across a boundary;
* boundary judgments;
* soft systems methodology;
* critical systems heuristics / boundary critique;
* lifecycle/system-of-interest framing in systems engineering.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* SEBoK, "System Boundary (glossary)", citing Checkland: "a distinction made by an observer..." https://sebokwiki.org/wiki/System_Boundary_%28glossary%29
* Peter Checkland, ''Systems Thinking, Systems Practice'', Wiley (1981; later 30-year retrospective edition). ISBN 9780471279112.
* Gerald Midgley, ''Systemic Intervention: Philosophy, Methodology, and Practice'', Kluwer/Plenum (2000). DOI/book record: 10.1007/978-1-4615-4201-8.
* Werner Ulrich and Martin Reynolds, "Critical Systems Heuristics: The Idea and Practice of Boundary Critique" (2020). DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4471-7472-1_6.
* ISO/IEC/IEEE 15288:2023, ''Systems and software engineering — System life cycle processes'', for system-of-interest engineering context.
* English Wikipedia/other general encyclopedic material only as orientation where available.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated systems-theory "System boundary" article found in the current search. Search results for mathematical boundary-value problems are unrelated and must not be treated as coverage of this concept.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* whether boundary critique belongs in the Core page or mostly Technical;
* how to connect physical boundaries (e.g. membrane) as examples without confusing them with the general analytical concept.

== Version control ==

'''Current corpus state:''' concise seed article; accurate but too Git-centric and historically thin.

'''Canonical scope:''' systematic recording, identification, comparison, branching/merging and retrieval of revisions of files or other versioned artifacts. It is broader than Git and is one component of software configuration management.

'''Important distinctions:'''
* version control ≠ backup;
* version control ≠ full software configuration management;
* repository history ≠ truth/correctness;
* centralized ≠ distributed version control;
* revision ≠ release;
* branch/merge models differ across systems.

'''Technical anchors:'''
* revision identifiers;
* snapshots versus deltas;
* repositories;
* branching and merging;
* conflicts;
* tags/releases;
* centralized and distributed architectures;
* provenance/attribution;
* reproducibility and audit trail;
* relation to configuration management.

'''Primary / authoritative source dossier:'''
* Marc J. Rochkind, "The Source Code Control System", ''IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering'' SE-1(4), 364–370 (1975). DOI: 10.1109/TSE.1975.6312866.
* Walter F. Tichy, "RCS—A System for Version Control", ''Software: Practice and Experience'' 15(7), 637–654 (1985). DOI: 10.1002/spe.4380150703.
* Scott Chacon and Ben Straub, ''Pro Git'', 2nd ed. (2014), especially "About Version Control"; current online edition maintained at git-scm.com.
* Git official documentation: Git is a distributed revision-control system; useful as an implementation source, not as the definition of version control in general.
* SWEBOK V4.0 for software configuration-management context.
* English Wikipedia, [[wikipedia:Version control|Version control]], useful history/terminology orientation only.

'''Scholarpedia:''' no dedicated general version-control article found in the current search.

'''Open questions for compile phase:'''
* whether provenance should be encoded as a cross-domain relationship rather than only discussed as an application;
* whether non-text/binary/data versioning is Core or Technical.

